"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][37]={"university_physics_en:M37-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M37-L1 – Stellar Structure and Hydrostatic Equilibrium<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M37 – Master: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6: continuum mechanics; M22:\nthermodynamics; M36: nuclear and particle physics · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M36&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain a spherically symmetric\nstellar pressure gradient and estimate it in a uniform-density toy\nstar.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A long-lived star is not a static ball of gas without energy\ntransport: its layers balance gravity, pressure, heat transport and\nenergy generation. Quasi-static models describe enclosed mass, pressure,\ntemperature and luminosity as radial functions. Nuclear reactions or\nother energy sources sustain thermal evolution, depending on the\nevolutionary stage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>In a thin spherical shell, an outward pressure-gradient force\nbalances inward gravity. For enclosed mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m(r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndensity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho(r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dP/dr=-Gm(r)\\rho(r)/r^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCombined with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dm/dr=4\\pi r^2\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthis is a coupled system. A constant-density sphere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=3M/(4\\pi R^3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith boundary condition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(R)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>G<\/mi><msup><mi>M<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_c=3GM^2/(8\\pi R^4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is an ideal mathematical comparison, not a realistic solar\nmodel.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{dP}{dr}=-\\frac{Gm(r)\\rho(r)}{r^2},\\qquad \\frac{dm}{dr}=4\\pi r^2\\rho(r).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For the constructed example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>30<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=2.0\\times10^{30}\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=7.0\\times10^8\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe constant-density sphere yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.33<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>14<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_c\\approx1.33\\times10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReal stars are strongly stratified; this is not a measured solar core\npressure. The graphic separates pressure support and gravitational pull\nin a notional shell.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_281_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic cross-section of a spherical star with a shell, radial pressure gradient and gravity.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic cross-section of a spherical\nstar with a shell, radial pressure gradient and gravity.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Spherical toy shell; arrows represent forces rather than measured\npressures.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What sign has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dP/dr<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m,\\rho,r&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a stellar model need an energy-transport relation in\naddition to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dP/dr<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndecreases by a factor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>16<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Negative: pressure decreases outward.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Without heat transport and temperature, the equation of state and\nstructure are not closed.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Hydrostatic balance assumes approximately slow evolution. Uniform\ndensity, no rotation and no magnetic fields are restrictive\nsimplifications; convection and radiative transport may dominate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Energy flow leads to accretion and compact objects. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://radio.astro.gla.ac.uk/stellarlect/index.html\">University of Glasgow – Stellar Astrophysics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course overview covering stellar structure, hydrostatic balance and energy transport; Subject relevance to lesson: M37-L1 – Stellar Structure and Hydrostatic Equilibrium · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://astrobytes-edu.github.io/astr201/lessons/lecture-05-stellar-structure-scalings/reading/04-stellar-blueprint/\">AstroBytes Education – The Stellar Blueprint<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Four coupled stellar-structure equations and their assumptions; example calculated independently; Subject relevance to lesson: M37-L1 – Stellar Structure and Hydrostatic Equilibrium · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M37-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M37-L2 – Compact Objects, Accretion and Radiative Power<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M37 – Master: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M29: curved spacetime; M37-L1:\nstellar structure · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish accretion power from\nthe Eddington limit and evaluate a simplified numerical example.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>White dwarfs, neutron stars and black holes have different surfaces\nand gravitational environments. During accretion, infalling matter can\nconvert gravitational energy to heat, radiation, outflows and motion.\nObserved luminosity depends not only on mass inflow but also on\ngeometry, radiative transfer and energy losses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>The simplified power relation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mover><mi>M<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\eta\\dot M c^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith mass accretion rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>M<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand radiative efficiency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA separate concept is the Eddington luminosity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_{\\mathrm Edd}=4\\pi GMm_pc/\\sigma_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor fully ionized hydrogen, Thomson scattering and spherical isotropic\nemission. It follows by balancing radiation force and gravity per\nelectron–proton pair; it is not a universal maximum for arbitrary\naccretion flows.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mover><mi>M<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\eta\\dot Mc^2,\\qquad L_{\\mathrm{Edd}}=\\frac{4\\pi GMm_pc}{\\sigma_T}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>M<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>14<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot M=10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>8.99<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>29<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L\\approx8.99\\times10^{29}\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Eddington scale for a hypothetical ten-solar-mass object under\npure-hydrogen assumptions is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.26<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>32<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.26\\times10^{32}\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is not a fit to a particular source.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_001_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic accretion disk around a compact object with inflowing mass and emitted radiative power.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic accretion disk around a compact\nobject with inflowing mass and emitted radiative power.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Simplified energy budget; disk and arrows are not to\nscale.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>M<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>M<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>28<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L\\approx9\\times10^{28}\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can collimation or anisotropic flows lead to apparent\nluminosities above\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_{\\mathrm{Edd}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Luminosity doubles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">About\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>13<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{13}\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The derivation assumes spherical isotropic radiation; collimated or\noptically thick flows violate that assumption.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The energy balance parametrizes efficiency, not detailed flow\nphysics. The Eddington relation assumes Thomson opacity; composition,\nrelativity, magnetic fields and disk geometry modify the comparison.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The next model links orbital speeds and galactic mass distribution.\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/523/2/1711/7179416\">Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society – Sustained Super-Eddington Accretion<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Context for flow geometry and limits of the Eddington model; no formula copied; Subject relevance to lesson: M37-L2 – Compact Objects, Accretion and Radiative Power · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M37-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M37-L3 – Galaxy Rotation and Dynamical Mass Profiles<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M37 – Master: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6: Newtonian dynamics; M37-L2:\ncompact sources · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Infer an enclosed mass profile\nfrom an idealized flat rotation curve and state the symmetry\nassumption.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Stars and gas in galaxies do not orbit solely under the gravity of\nvisible stars. Observed rotation curves are important evidence for\nadditional gravitating matter or modified dynamics, but their\ninterpretation requires explicit modelling. Real galactic disks are not\nspherical; the following equation first describes a spherical comparison\nmodel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For circular motion around a spherical enclosed mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(&lt;r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_c^2/r=GM(&lt;r)/r^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(&lt;r)=rv_c^2/G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_c(r)\\approx v_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover some radial range, the enclosed mass in this spherical model grows\napproximately linearly with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis differs from the Keplerian decline beyond a finite centrally\nconcentrated mass.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>G<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(spherical circular-orbit model)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(&lt;r)=\\frac{rv_c^2(r)}{G}\\qquad\\text{(spherical circular-orbit model)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_c=200\\,\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=10\\,\\mathrm{kpc}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none finds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.85<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>41<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.30<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mo>⊙<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(&lt;r)\\approx1.85\\times10^{41}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\approx9.30\\times10^{10}M_\\odot<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt the same orbital speed,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,\\mathrm{kpc}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwould imply twice the enclosed model mass. The figure compares a flat\ncurve with a Keplerian decline; it is not a galaxy data set.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_108_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Comparison of a flat galaxy rotation curve with a declining Keplerian model curve.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of a flat galaxy rotation\ncurve with a declining Keplerian model curve.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic rotation curves with illustrative orbital\nspeeds.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The curves are not observations and do not claim a universal\nshape.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(&lt;r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles at constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(&lt;r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why should the spherical formula not be applied unchanged to every\ndisk galaxy?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It doubles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It quadruples.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Disk mass distributions and their gravitational fields are not\nspherical, and gas motions may be non-circular.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The formula assumes circular orbits, Newtonian gravity and spherical\nsymmetry. Inclination, bars, turbulence, stellar mass and resolution\naffect real fits. Dark matter is an interpretation supported by multiple\nobservations, not a directly measured quantity in this exercise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>From galaxy scales we turn to the early Universe and its relic\nradiation. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/485/1/934/5318639\">Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society – The Shapes of Galaxy Rotation Curves<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Observational background for rotation curves and model limitations; Subject relevance to lesson: M37-L3 – Galaxy Rotation and Dynamical Mass Profiles · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M37-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M37-L4 – Cosmic Microwave Background, Recombination and Decoupling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M37 – Master: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M29: FLRW spacetime; M37-L3: galaxies\n· <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate the temperature\nscaling of the CMB and distinguish recombination from last\nscattering.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>The cosmic microwave background (CMB) is an almost thermal relic\nradiation field. Early photons scattered frequently from free electrons\nin an ionized plasma. As the Universe cooled, neutral atoms formed more\nreadily (recombination); the declining free-electron fraction allowed\nphotons to travel longer distances without scattering (photon\ndecoupling). Recombination and last scattering are physically linked,\nbut neither instantaneous nor exactly simultaneous.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Under adiabatic expansion of an ideal photon gas, temperature scales\nas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\propto a^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor cosmological scale factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+z=a_0/a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(z)=T_0(1+z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLast scattering is often described broadly as occurring around\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>380<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">380,000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyears after the hot beginning at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mn>1100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\sim1100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nYet the visibility function distributes last-scattering events across a\nfinite time/redshift interval; temperature scaling alone does not yield\na precise decoupling time.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>a<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(z)=T_0(1+z),\\qquad 1+z=\\frac{a_0}{a}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Using an illustrative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.725<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_0=2.725\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=1100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.725<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1101<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3000.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2.725\\cdot1101\\,\\mathrm K\\approx3000.2\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis illustrates radiation temperature at high redshift; atomic kinetics\ncontrols the actual ionization history. The graphic shows overlapping\ntransitions rather than a single sharp instant.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_042_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Timeline schematic: ionized plasma, gradual recombination and a finite distribution of last-scattering events.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Timeline schematic: ionized plasma,\ngradual recombination and a finite distribution of last-scattering\nevents.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Recombination and decoupling overlap but are not an identical\nsingle event.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the temperature at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>999<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=999<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.725<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_0=2.725\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does a photon wavelength change if the scale factor\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is one exact instant for last scattering misleading?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2725<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2725\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It doubles under cosmological redshifting of freely propagating\nphotons.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Scattering probability and neutral fraction change continuously over\na finite epoch.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The 380,000-year age is an approximate model statement, not a sharp\nmeasured phase boundary. Later reionization can scatter photons again.\nCMB anisotropies include Doppler, gravitational and projection\neffects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The CMB fluctuations help constrain the ingredients of cosmological\nmodels. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://lambda.gsfc.nasa.gov/education/graphic_history/microwaves.html\">NASA LAMBDA – Cosmic Microwave Background<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Last scattering and approximate 380,000-year epoch; not evidence of instantaneous transitions; Subject relevance to lesson: M37-L4 – Cosmic Microwave Background, Recombination and Decoupling · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Planck/Planck_science_highlights\">ESA – Planck Science Highlights<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · CMB anisotropies and cosmological parameters in the Planck context; Subject relevance to lesson: M37-L4 – Cosmic Microwave Background, Recombination and Decoupling · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/529/3/2274/7630233\">Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society – Angular Correlations of CMB Spectrum Distortions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Finite duration of recombination/last scattering and the visibility function; Subject relevance to lesson: M37-L4 – Cosmic Microwave Background, Recombination and Decoupling · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M37-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M37-L5 – Dark Matter, Dark Energy and Cosmic Expansion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M37 – Master: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M29: general relativity and FLRW;\nM37-L4: CMB · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute the expansion rate at a\nselected redshift in a simple flat matter–Lambda model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Cosmic expansion is described by the Hubble rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=\\dot a/a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStandard cosmological models treat baryonic and cold dark matter as\nmatter components with similar mean density scaling, while a\ncosmological constant has constant energy density. Dark matter and dark\nenergy are distinct model ingredients; the Friedmann equation does not\nestablish their microscopic nature.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>In an FLRW model, the terms in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msubsup><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z)^2/H_0^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscale according to equation of state: radiation as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+z)^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnonrelativistic matter as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+z)^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncurvature as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+z)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a cosmological constant as a constant. For an idealized flat\nlate-time model without radiation,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega_{m0}+\\Omega_{\\Lambda0}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z)=H_0\\sqrt{\\Omega_{m0}(1+z)^3+\\Omega_{\\Lambda0}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a model-dependent prediction, not a direct measurement of dark\ncomponents’ microphysics.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(flat matter–<\/mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> toy model)<\/mtext><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{H(z)}{H_0}=\\sqrt{\\Omega_{m0}(1+z)^3+\\Omega_{\\Lambda0}}\\quad\\text{(flat matter--}\\Lambda\\text{ toy model)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For specified\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega_{m0}=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega_{\\Lambda0}=0.7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none obtains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>3.1<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.761<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H/H_0=\\sqrt{0.3\\cdot8+0.7}=\\sqrt{3.1}\\approx1.761<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H/H_0=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby construction. The figure plots relative contributions under the\nassumed model, not new measurements.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_053_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Model comparison of matter density increasing with redshift and a constant Lambda component.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Model comparison of matter density\nincreasing with redshift and a constant Lambda component.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Model contributions to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nduring cosmic expansion.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The figure does not show measurements or establish the microphysical\nnature of dark components.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(2)/H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the same parameters.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which term dominates at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>≫<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\gg1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin this simplified matter–Lambda model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must the approximation not be extrapolated unchanged into the\nradiation era?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>8.8<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.966<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{8.8}\\approx2.966<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The matter term proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+z)^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Radiation and the evolving cosmic inventory become important.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Parameters are illustrative rounded values, not a specific Planck\nfit. Radiation and curvature were omitted; observational probes can have\ntensions and systematic uncertainties. A cosmological constant does not\nrepresent all models of dark energy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Different messengers can probe the same cosmic processes in\ncomplementary ways. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.esa.int/cs/articles/about-us/directorates/science/planck/planck-and-the-cosmic-microwave-background\">ESA – Planck and the Cosmic Microwave Background<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Description of cosmological model parameters and complementary evidence; Subject relevance to lesson: M37-L5 – Dark Matter, Dark Energy and Cosmic Expansion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Planck/Planck_science_highlights\">ESA – Planck Science Highlights<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · CMB anisotropies and cosmological parameters in the Planck context; Subject relevance to lesson: M37-L5 – Dark Matter, Dark Energy and Cosmic Expansion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M37-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M37-L6 – Multimessenger Astronomy and Chance Coincidences<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M37 – Master: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M36: particle and count detection;\nM37-L5: cosmological observations · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate the expected\nchance-coincidence count for independent stationary triggers and\ndistinguish it from source identification.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Electromagnetic radiation, neutrinos and gravitational waves carry\ndifferent information about astrophysical events. A time coincidence\nacross detectors is a clue, not proof of a common source. Direction,\ntiming, significance, background and selection effects must be assessed\ntogether. Real-time alerts coordinate follow-up observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For two independent stationary Poisson trigger streams of rates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na symmetric time cut\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|t_A-t_B|\\le\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover observing duration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives approximate expected accidental pairs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{\\mathrm{chance}}\\simeq2\\Delta t\\,R_AR_BT<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen windows are short and rates stationary. This counts event pairs,\nnot necessarily independent physical events; sky positions are not used.\nOverlapping windows or time-varying rates require more careful\nanalysis.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(independent stationary triggers)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{\\mathrm{chance}}\\simeq2\\,\\Delta t\\,R_A R_B T\\qquad\\text{(independent stationary triggers)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Assume\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_A=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_B=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntriggers per day,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=100\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>365<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=365<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndays. In common day units the approximation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>86400<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>365<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4.23<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{\\mathrm{chance}}=2(100/86400)\\cdot1\\cdot5\\cdot365\\approx4.23<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchance pairs per observing year. This is a constructed statistics\nproblem, not an IceCube result. The original diagram shows three\ninformation channels and a coincidence window.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_186_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic light, neutrino and gravitational-wave channels with a time-coincidence analysis window.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic light, neutrino and\ngravitational-wave channels with a time-coincidence analysis\nwindow.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Different messengers and a joint time check; not a record of any\nactual detection.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{\\mathrm{chance}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis halved?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What expectation results when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_B=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrather than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>5<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper day?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a temporal match without direction not proof of one\nsource?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">About\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2.11<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.11<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npairs.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">About\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>8.45<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8.45<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npairs.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Independent background triggers can fall in the same time window by\nchance.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The Poisson model assumes stationary independent streams and\nnegligible boundary/selection effects. Real multimessenger analyses\ncombine energy, sky-location uncertainty, exposure, detector sensitivity\nand trial corrections. The toy result assigns no actual astrophysical\nsource.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Gravitational deflection of electromagnetic signals supplies another\ngeometric probe. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://icecube.wisc.edu/science/real-time-alerts/\">IceCube Neutrino Observatory – Real-Time Alerts<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Multimessenger alerts and neutrino follow-up; does not verify the toy coincidence rate; Subject relevance to lesson: M37-L6 – Multimessenger Astronomy and Chance Coincidences · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M37-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M37-L7 – Gravitational Lensing and Einstein-Ring Geometry<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M37 – Master: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M29: relativity and light deflection;\nM37-L6: observing channels · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate the Einstein angle of\nan idealized point lens and explain the distance definitions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A massive foreground object bends light from a background source and\nmay produce distorted multiple images. Perfect axial alignment with an\naxisymmetric point lens gives an Einstein ring. Its angular scale\ndepends on lens mass and geometry. Real galaxy lenses are extended and\nmay contain shear and other mass components.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>In the thin-lens, weak-field point-mass approximation, the Einstein\nangle is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_E=\\sqrt{(4GM/c^2)\\,D_{LS}/(D_LD_S)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the observer–lens angular-diameter distance,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe observer–source distance and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{LS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe lens–source angular-diameter distance; they cannot generally be\nsubtracted as Euclidean distances in an expanding cosmology. This\nrelation alone does not reconstruct a galaxy mass distribution.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mfrac><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(point lens)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_E=\\sqrt{\\frac{4GM}{c^2}\\frac{D_{LS}}{D_L D_S}}\\qquad\\text{(point lens)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a hypothetical point mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>11<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mo>⊙<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=10^{11}M_\\odot<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand assigned values interpreted as angular-diameter distances\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_L=1\\,\\mathrm{Gpc}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_S=2\\,\\mathrm{Gpc}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{LS}=1\\,\\mathrm{Gpc}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none finds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.09<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.64<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_E\\approx3.09\\,\\mu\\mathrm{rad}\\approx0.64\\,\\mathrm{arcsec}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA realistic cosmology requires all three distances to be obtained\nconsistently from one model. The figure depicts ideal ring\nformation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_118_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic light rays for an aligned point lens with foreground mass, background source and Einstein-ring image-plane cross-section.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic light rays for an aligned point\nlens with foreground mass, background source and Einstein-ring\nimage-plane cross-section.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Ideal lens model.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Neither a real telescope image nor an inferred galaxy mass map.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscale if the mass quadruples at fixed distances?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens mathematically for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{LS}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the point-lens model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are three arbitrary independent distances not automatically\nconsistent with a cosmological model?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The angle doubles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_E=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Angular-diameter distances are linked by spacetime geometry and\nredshifts.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The point-lens relation assumes weak deflection, a thin lens and\nideal alignment. Extended lenses, microlensing, multiple planes and\noff-axis positions alter the images. Numerical values are a constructed\ndimensional comparison only.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>M37 now connects stellar models, galaxy dynamics, the early Universe\nand observing channels.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://esahubble.org/wordbank/gravitational-lensing/\">ESA/Hubble – Gravitational Lensing<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Light deflection, lens morphology and applications; Subject relevance to lesson: M37-L7 – Gravitational Lensing and Einstein-Ring Geometry · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5567250/\">Living Reviews in Relativity – Gravitational Lensing in Astronomy<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Einstein rings and the point-lens approximation as technical context; Subject relevance to lesson: M37-L7 – Gravitational Lensing and Einstein-Ring Geometry · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
